; The zone the fleet serves, and the one spex write into.
;
; `.test` deliberately, not `.com`. RFC 6761 reserves it for exactly this and
; guarantees it will never be delegated, so a record left here cannot collide
; with a real name and a lookup that escapes the fleet fails instead of
; reaching somebody's live domain.
;
; The serial is not maintained by hand: CoreDNS's `reload` watches the file's
; mtime and content, so writing the file is enough.
$ORIGIN astralbi.test.
$TTL 1

@       IN  SOA ns.astralbi.test. hostmaster.astralbi.test. (
            1786465759          ; serial
            60         ; refresh
            60         ; retry
            600        ; expire
            1 )        ; minimum

@       IN  NS  ns.astralbi.test.
ns      IN  A   127.0.0.1
probe   IN  TXT "hello-from-the-fleet"
